@h1 What is Wit? h1@

Wit is a plain-text markup language for documents. Ordinary sentences render as
ordinary paragraphs. When you need a heading, a list, a table, a figure, or a
citation, you add a node for that one thing, and the rest of the text stays as
it was. One source file compiles to HTML, Markdown, or print-ready PDF.

@h2 A first look h2@

This is a complete document. The only markup is the part that isn't prose:

@@@pre
@h1 Field notes — week three h1@

The tide gauge read *2.4 metres* at dawn, higher than any
figure in the log.

@figure
  @img |src ./gauge.png| |alt The tide gauge at dawn|
  @figcaption Thursday's reading, before the wind turned. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

Compile it to HTML and you get an @@@code <h1> code@@@, a paragraph carrying a
@@@code <strong> code@@@, and a real @@@code <figure> code@@@ with its
@@@code <figcaption> code@@@. Compile the same source to Markdown and the heading
becomes @code # Field notes code@ and the emphasis becomes @code **2.4 metres** code@.

@h2 Nodes: structure where you ask for it h2@

A @strong node strong@ is a named structure, written @@@code @name … name@ code@@@.
Headings, lists, tables, figures, asides, and sectioning are built in — you use
them with no setup, and the renderer decides whether each is a block or an inline
span. Everything outside a node stays prose. The full set is in
@a |href /docs/start/nodes/| Nodes and core vocabulary a@.

@h2 Data, when a document repeats itself h2@

A fact can live as data instead of being retyped. A @strong record strong@ is
@@@code { key - value } code@@@; pull a field into a sentence with
@@@code @name.field code@@@:

@@@pre
#station: { name - Ledbury Point, keeper - A. Vane }

Readings this week come from @station.name, logged by @station.keeper.
pre@@@

That renders @strong Readings this week come from Ledbury Point, logged by
A. Vane. strong@ Write the fact once and reference it wherever it belongs. When a
document has to assemble itself from a list of records — a table of readings, a
bibliography, a cast of characters — collections and iteration handle that too;
@a |href /docs/start/mental-model/| the mental model a@ shows how prose, nodes,
data, and logic stack up.

@h2 What ships h2@

Wit is five zero-dependency TypeScript packages — the parser, the runtime, an
HTML renderer, a Markdown renderer, and the @code wit code@ command-line tool —
plus a VS Code extension. A clean install pulls in nothing else. Write with the
@code wit code@ command and you need never see the packages; embed the packages
in your own program and you need never touch the command line.

@h2 What Wit is not h2@

Wit is a file format, not a content-management system, an editor, or a general
templating engine. It is deliberately @strong not Turing-complete strong@: there
are conditionals and loops for shaping a document from its data, and one scripting
block as the explicit escape hatch for anything more. It is also young — the
syntax is stable enough to write a book in, but the ecosystem is small. For fine
typesetting control over kerning and page floats, reach for LaTeX; to write in
plain text and add structure only where you need it, reach for Wit.

